Here's your RHEL 8 health check script. Here's what it covers across 11 sections:
Section
What's Checked
OS & Kernel
RHEL version, kernel, uptime
CPU
Usage %, I/O wait %, load average vs core count
RAM
Used/available GB, usage %, buffers & cache
Swap
Used/free GB, usage %, vm.swappiness tuning
Disk / Hard Disk
Per-filesystem space %, inode usage, block devices, I/O stats (iostat)
Filesystem
Read-only detection, /tmp write test, XFS info
Network
Interface UP/DOWN states, IP addresses
Services
sshd, chronyd, firewalld, rsyslog, etc. + failed units
Open Files
ulimit -n, file descriptor usage vs system max
Security/Updates
SELinux mode, firewalld, pending dnf updates, last logins
Kernel Errors
Recent dmesg critical/error messages
Thresholds (configurable at the top):
CPU: warn ≥70%, critical ≥90%
RAM: warn ≥75%, critical ≥90%
Swap: warn ≥50%, critical ≥80%
Disk: warn ≥75%, critical ≥90%

# Run interactively with color output
sudo bash rhel8_healthcheck.sh

# Save a plain-text report
sudo bash rhel8_healthcheck.sh --report /var/log/healthcheck_$(date +%F).txt

# Schedule via cron (daily at 6 AM)
echo "0 6 * * * root bash /opt/rhel8_healthcheck.sh --report /var/log/healthcheck_\$(date +\%F).txt" >> /etc/cron.d/healthcheck

Note: Install sysstat (dnf install sysstat) for disk I/O stats via iostat. The script runs fine without it but will show a warning for that check.
